{
  "answer": {
    "forms": "Two slots (0x64b9b70, 0x64ba390) are their own vtable base: the reader loads the slot address itself, and the slot begins a run of seven consecutive code entries.  The other five slots are the first -- and only -- code entry of a vtable whose {drop, size, align} header sits in the 0x18 bytes before the slot (the entry after each of the five points into .rodata: 0x448bd0 for three of them, 0x4349eb for two), and the reader loads the header address.",
    "no_other_path": "No relocation addend, no stored 8-byte copy outside the relocation sections, no immediate materialisation, and no dynamic symbol names any of the seven slots or five bases.",
    "one_reader_each": "Each of the seven stored pointers is read through exactly one materialisation of its vtable base: seven `lea` reference sites in seven distinct reader bodies, and no other rip-relative reference to any slot or base exists.",
    "uses": "Five readers store the materialised base into a record they construct on the stack -- at field offset 0x10 of a 0x200-byte record for the two slot-base readers, and at field offset 0x30 of a 0x128-byte record for the three header-form constructors -- and the record is then copied into a fresh allocation.  The remaining two readers (the header-form dispatchers) also store the base, into an object field at +0x70, and immediately read the stored pointer back with `call qword ptr [rax + 0x18]`: the effective address of that call is the slot itself, so these two bodies are direct readers of the stored pointer, calling exactly the body it names (0x2a225c0 and 0x2a2a0e0)."
  },
